---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HireOrNah from "../../components/fun/HireOrNah";
import { SAMPLE_JOBS } from "../../data/fun/jobs";
import "../../components/fun/notebook.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Hire or Nah | Jun Wei Ng"
  description="Paste a job description and Jev judges Jun Wei's resume against it, or bring your own job description and resume."
  sideBarActiveItemID="fun"
>
  <article class="nb">
    <div class="nb-sheet">
      <header class="nb-head">
        <p class="nb-eyebrow"><a href="/fun">Fun stuff</a> · entry 02</p>
        <h1>Hire or <em>Nah</em></h1>
        <p class="nb-dek">
          Jev is basically a model for making judgement calls. Give it a job description and a resume, and it picks one of five
          verdicts from Strong Hire to No Hire. It also scores the match on skills, seniority, domain fit and whether the person has
          actually shipped anything.
        </p>
      </header>

      <HireOrNah client:load jobs={SAMPLE_JOBS} />

      <aside class="nb-about">
        <p class="nb-label">About the model</p>
        <h2>So what is a System One model?</h2>
        <p>
          Most language models generate an answer one token at a time. Jev only makes decisions from options you define up front.
          For this page, those options are the five hiring verdicts. It returns a probability for each one together with the smaller
          checks below, so it cannot suddenly go off and write a motivational speech instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://typesafe.ai/blog/introducing-system-one-models-and-jev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jev</a> is the first
          public model from TypeSafe AI. They report response times of 70 to 500 milliseconds for System One tasks and do not charge
          for output tokens. The result here takes a little longer because I liked the fake deliberation animation too much.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then <a href="https://laya.convaiinnovations.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Laya</a> showed up a few days later. It is
          an open-source alternative built from research its creator published in March 2025, and it can run on a free Colab GPU.
          Apparently the race to build the world's fastest boring decision-maker is now a thing.
        </p>
        <p class="nb-mono nb-links">
          <a href="https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction/quickstart" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jev docs</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/NandhaKishorM/laya" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Laya on GitHub</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
